This page sets out the problem NetScript solves, what it changes, and what it costs, so you can
decide whether it fits. If the product is a static site, a short-lived prototype, or one
request/response process, start with Fresh or Hono. NetScript starts to pay for itself when the
boundaries between processes, background work, and the browser have become part of the product.

## The integration tax

Assembling a backend from libraries that were never designed together has a standing cost.
Choosing them happens once; maintaining the seams between them never stops:

- **The service and the browser disagree.** Request and response shapes drift until a production
  call finds the mismatch.
- **A retry loop stands in for durable state.** Multi-step workflows have no recorded checkpoints
  or explicit rollbacks, so a process that dies leaves the system half-executed.
- **Resources and telemetry are assembled separately.** Queue, service, database, health, and
  trace configuration drift, so an incident crosses several tools before anyone can explain it.

That standing maintenance is the **integration tax**: the cost of making unrelated tools behave as
one system.

## What a meta-framework changes

NetScript does not replace the underlying primitives: Fresh renders, Hono serves, and Postgres
stores as they would without it. It standardizes the boundaries that become expensive once they
spread through an application:

- **Shared contracts** keep handlers and callers aligned, and services serve the contract at
  request time.
- **Durable runtimes** in plugins make work that outlives a request explicit.
- **Generated resource wiring** keeps processes, dependencies, and telemetry in one operational
  model, and Aspire starts the resources with their telemetry connected.

These constraints cost flexibility. In return, a team doesn't redesign the same seams in every
service. [Core concepts](/concepts/) covers how each layer works.

## How it compares

No option wins everywhere. Choose the one whose main constraint matches yours.

| Option | Choose it when | How NetScript differs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fresh or Hono | The web app or small API is the whole system. | NetScript uses Fresh and Hono as foundations, then adds service contracts, plugin runtimes, and an Aspire workspace. The cost is more framework and more project shape. |
| Next.js | React and the Next ecosystem are the center. | NetScript chooses Deno with Fresh and Preact, plus explicit service and durable-runtime boundaries. It is not the React/Next path; choose the web ecosystem your product needs. |
| Encore-style stack | An integrated infrastructure workflow is the center. | NetScript keeps a Deno/JSR workspace in the application and uses Aspire for local orchestration through an adapter. It provides less managed infrastructure, so deployment and operations remain your responsibility. |
| Temporal | A dedicated workflow platform is the main decision. | NetScript's saga core keeps TypeScript definitions, store and bus ports, transports, and compensation inside a broader application workspace. It is not a drop-in Temporal API. |
| Assemble it yourself | Maximum component choice is a requirement. | You can choose every queue, tracer, service layer, and runtime independently. NetScript constrains those choices; assembling them yourself leaves every integration seam with your team. |

[Comparisons](/comparisons/) puts NetScript code side by side with frontend and backend frameworks.
[How NetScript's path compares](/explanation/compared/) covers what the getting-started flows of
Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit, Laravel, and Rails teach first, where NetScript's order differs, and where
those peers are ahead today.

## Trade-offs

- **The package family is pre-1.0.** APIs move, so scaffolded imports use exact
  `{{ releaseSpecifier }}` pins. If you need a frozen surface today, NetScript is not ready for you.
- **The Aspire CLI is the default orchestration path.** Scaffold with `--no-aspire` to opt out;
  resource wiring and local telemetry then belong to you. [See the Aspire boundary](/quickstart/aspire/).
- **The web layer is Fresh and Preact.** That is a deliberate fit, not compatibility with the
  React/Next ecosystem. [See the web layer](/web-layer/).
- **A coordinated workspace adds structure that a one-process app may never use.** Durability,
  plugin runtimes, and multi-resource operations justify that cost only when the product needs
  them.
- **NetScript is not a hosted platform.** You own the workspace and the deployment decisions.

